nel Requirement | Tested Version | Dependencies |\n| --------------- | ------------------ | -------------- | ------------ |\n| **Ubuntu 22**   | ≥5.15.0            | 22.4           | fuse3-3.10.5 |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1900":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","type":"page","content":"Technical Architecture","breadcrumbs":["Documents","Overview"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/architecture","locale":"en"},"1901":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-0","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Architecture Overview","locale":"en"},"1902":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-1","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-performance-design","content":"High-Performance Design","locale":"en"},"1903":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-2","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-availability-design","content":"High Availability Design","locale":"en"},"1904":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-3","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture","content":"This chapter provides an in-depth introduction to Curvine's technical architecture, detailing the functionality, interaction patterns, and design principles of each layer component to help you comprehensively understand how the Curvine system works.","locale":"en"},"1905":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-4","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Curvine adopts a layered distributed architecture design that ensures clear component responsibilities with excellent scalability and high availability. The entire architecture is primarily divided into three layers: **control layer** (Master), **storage layer** (Workers), and **access layer** (clients: FUSE, SDK, CLI), which collaborate to complete data storage, processing, and management tasks.","locale":"en"},"1906":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-5","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Curvine consists of three main server-side roles plus clients and UFS:","locale":"en"},"1907":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-6","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Curvine Client**: Data read/write operations are implemented by the client, which calls Curvine server-side interfaces through RPC (metadata from Master, block data from Workers). The client supports multiple access methods:","locale":"en"},"1908":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-7","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**FUSE Mount**: POSIX-compatible mount via libfuse/3 (curvine-fuse)","locale":"en"},"1909":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-8","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**HDFS & SDK**: Java / Python / libsdk interfaces","locale":"en"},"1910":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-9","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**CSI Driver**: Kubernetes volume driver (curvine-csi)","locale":"en"},"1911":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-10","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**CLI (`cv`)**: Admin tools for fs ops, report, and cluster management","locale":"en"},"1912":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-11","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Internally, the client layer is composed of:","locale":"en"},"1913":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-12","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**UnifiedFileSystem**: Provides unified namespace with CurvineFS + UFS fallback","locale":"en"},"1914":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-13","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**FsReader / FsWriter**: Parallel, buffered I/O with zero-copy optimization","locale":"en"},"1915":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-14","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**BlockClient Pool**: Manages block_reader / block_writer for local and remote access","locale":"en"},"1916":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-15","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Mount & Meta Cache**: Caches mount info and inode metadata","locale":"en"},"1917":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-16","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Master**: The core control node; responsible for metadata (directory tree, file inodes, block locations), Worker registration, block allocation, and UFS mount table. Master can run as multiple nodes forming a Raft group for high availability; only the Raft leader serves metadata writes.","locale":"en"},"1918":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-17","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"The Master cluster mainly includes:","locale":"en"},"1919":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-18","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"tex