":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway","content":"The Curvine S3 gateway exposes a Curvine cluster through an S3-compatible HTTP API. Tools that already speak S3 — the AWS CLI, MinIO Client, boto3, or any S3 SDK — can read and write Curvine paths without FUSE or a native Curvine client. The gateway authenticates each request with AWS Signature V2/V4 and translates bucket and key names into Curvine filesystem operations.","locale":"en"},"2171":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway-40","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway","content":"The gateway is an optional, standalone service. It is not started by `curvine-server` and is not included in the default release package yet. Build it explicitly with `cargo build --release --bin curvine-s3-gateway` and run it alongside a Curvine cluster.","locale":"en"},"2172":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway-41","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway#overview","content":"S3 requests enter the gateway, are authenticated against a credential store, and are executed through the Curvine client. Object data streams through the gateway to and from Curvine Workers, so an entire object is never buffered in memory.","locale":"en"},"2173":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway-42","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway#key-features","content":"S3-compatible bucket and object APIs (see Supported S3 Operations)","locale":"en"},"2174":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway-43","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway#key-features","content":"AWS Signature V2/V4 authentication with a local or Curvine-hosted credential store","locale":"en"},"2175":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/Access/s3-gateway-44","page_id"